LESSONS_P4 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The quotation cycle: scope and position", "11", "<p>This module covers Requests for Quotation, Supplier Quotations, and the comparison cycle in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: state when the policy requires competitive quotation; complete the Request for Quotation walk and issue it to suppliers; enter Supplier Quotations from source documents; build the comparison worksheet with landed figures; run the award decision with its criteria and record; conduct the negotiation round and establish standing arrangements; and run the cycle reads.</p><p><b>What the cycle is.</b> The quotation cycle converts screened demand into priced supply options and a recorded award decision: the business asks named suppliers what they would charge, records what each answered, compares the answers on stated criteria, and decides — with the decision, its reasoning, and its alternatives filed. The cycle's product is not only a price: it is <b>the negotiation memory</b> module 2's buying lists draw their sourced rates from, and the record every later price conversation is conducted against.</p><p><b>When the cycle is required.</b> Per the written procurement policy:</p><p>1. <b>By value</b> — purchases above the policy's threshold require competitive quotes (the number of quotes per band stated in the policy).<br>2. <b>By class</b> — the classes the policy names (new items, capital equipment, annual arrangements) quote regardless of single-purchase value, because the arrangement's total exposure is the relevant figure.<br>3. <b>The exceptions, named</b> — single-source items (the sole manufacturer of a registered product), genuine emergencies through the expedite lane, and repeat purchases inside a standing arrangement's validity — each exception recorded with its reason, because an unrecorded exception is indistinguishable from an avoided control.</p><p><b>Position in the flow.</b> Demand (module 3) precedes the cycle; the cycle's award feeds the Purchase Order (module 5) and the buying lists (module 2); the supplier's later performance (module 8's scorecards) feeds the next cycle's comparison. The sequence gives procurement its accountability shape: <b>every rate on an order traces to a quotation or arrangement, and every award can state why it was made.</b></p><p><b>Custody.</b> The procurement function runs the cycle; award decisions approve per the matrix's bands; the comparison and decision records file per the one-lookup standard; and the standing rule: <b>a quoted rate is evidence — entered from the supplier's document, compared landed, awarded by stated criteria, and filed with its alternatives.</b></p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the Request for Quotation. Chapter 3: Supplier Quotation entry. Chapter 4: the comparison worksheet. Chapter 5: the award decision. Chapter 6: negotiation and arrangements. Chapter 7: the cycle reads. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Request for Quotation", "12", "<p>Open Request for Quotation (awesome bar) &gt; New. Complete the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. The suppliers table.</b> The named suppliers the request goes to — selected from the register (module 1's gate is the entry point; the cycle does not quote unregistered parties), per the policy's count for the band, drawn from the classes the item belongs to. Supplier selection is itself recorded reasoning: the incumbents, the qualified alternatives, and — where the read shows the same short list every cycle — the deliberate widening the competitiveness review directs.</p><p><b>2. The items table.</b> Drawn from the driving Material Requests (the link carries the demand trace through the cycle):</p><p>1. <b>Item, quantity, and unit</b> — the consolidated quantities from module 3's worksheet, in the purchase units, because rates will be quoted against these quantities and volume terms depend on them.<br>2. <b>Required-by schedule</b> — the dates the demand stated: suppliers quote lead times against real dates.<br>3. <b>Warehouse</b> — the delivery destination, because delivered-to-where is part of what is being priced (the supplier's included delivery versus ex-works collection changes the landed comparison).</p><p><b>3. The message to suppliers.</b> The request's terms: the response deadline, the validity period expected on quotes, the requirement to state price breaks, minimums, lead times, and payment terms, and the specification references for items that carry them. A request that does not ask for breaks and terms receives quotes that omit them, and the comparison then runs on incomplete columns — the request's completeness determines the comparison's.</p><p><b>4. Issuing the request.</b> The request sends per the site's channels — the emailed document, or the supplier portal link where the site operates portal quoting (suppliers entering their own rates against the request, which lands the responses as draft Supplier Quotations directly). Either channel, the same record rule: what was asked, of whom, by when, filed on the request.</p><p><b>5. The response window.</b> The deadline is managed, not hoped: the reminder at the mid-point to non-responders, the response log as quotes arrive, and the close at the deadline — late quotes admitted only by the recorded exception (the policy states when), because a comparison whose window silently extends for one supplier is not the level comparison the file claims. Non-response is itself data: the response-rate read (Chapter 7) accumulates it per supplier, and module 8's relationship review consumes it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Supplier Quotation entry", "12", "<p>Each response is recorded as a Supplier Quotation. This chapter is the entry discipline.</p><p><b>1. The Supplier Quotation doctype.</b> Open Supplier Quotation (awesome bar) — or receive it from the portal as a draft. The record per responding supplier:</p><p>1. <b>The supplier and the request reference</b> — the link to the Request for Quotation carrying the cycle's thread.<br>2. <b>The items table</b> — the quoted rate per item per unit, the quoted lead time, and the quantity terms.<br>3. <b>Validity</b> — the date the quote holds until: the field the award clock runs against, and the field the re-quote cadence (Chapter 6) watches.<br>4. <b>Terms</b> — payment terms offered, delivery terms (included delivery or ex-works — the landed comparison's input), and any conditions the supplier attaches.</p><p><b>2. The entry discipline.</b> Quotes entered by the procurement function follow the evidence rule:</p><p>1. <b>From the supplier's document</b> — the emailed quotation, the price circular, the signed offer — attached to the Supplier Quotation. The record states what the supplier stated; the attachment proves it. A rate entered from a phone call is entered with the call noted and confirmed in writing before the comparison closes, because an award justified by an unverifiable rate is an award that cannot defend itself.<br>2. <b>The pairing rule at entry</b> — the quoted rate belongs to a quoted unit: ₦9,600 per Carton-12 entered as such, never transcribed onto a unit line. Transcription is where comparison errors are born, and the entry check reads quantity-unit-rate together per the standing discipline.<br>3. <b>Breaks entered as breaks</b> — a supplier quoting tiered rates (one rate to 199 cartons, a better rate from 200, better again from 300) has each tier recorded, because the comparison at the consolidated quantity needs the tier that quantity lands in, and the consolidation arithmetic (module 3) may deliberately cross a tier.</p><p><b>3. Foreign-currency quotes.</b> The Indian manufacturer quotes in dollars: the Supplier Quotation carries the quoted currency and rate as quoted, and the comparison worksheet (Chapter 4) does the conversion at the planning rate — the quote records the supplier's statement; the worksheet does the arithmetic. Converting at entry buries the exposure the comparison must show.</p><p><b>4. Clarifications.</b> Ambiguities — a quote silent on delivery terms, a break structure unclear — are resolved by written question and answer filed with the quotation, before comparison. A comparison built on assumed readings of ambiguous quotes produces awards the supplier will dispute at the first invoice.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The comparison worksheet", "13", "<p>Recorded quotes compare on one worksheet. This chapter is its columns and its rules.</p><p><b>1. The worksheet's shape.</b> Per item (or per lot where items award together): one row per quoting supplier, columns per criterion, the consolidated quantity from module 3 as the comparison quantity, and the file reference per row to the Supplier Quotation it summarises. The worksheet is a record, not a scratchpad: it files with the decision, and the trace audit expects to find it.</p><p><b>2. The landed column — the comparison's centre.</b> Per module 2's comparison rule, suppliers compare landed, never ex-works:</p><p>1. <b>Domestic quotes with included delivery</b> — the quoted rate is close to the landed figure; the worksheet notes any excluded charges.<br>2. <b>Ex-works and foreign quotes</b> — the landed worksheet (module 2, Chapter 7) computes the full figure: goods value converted at the planning rate, plus freight, insurance, duties, clearing, and haulage. The Indian manufacturer's attractive dollar rate is compared only after it becomes a naira-per-unit-on-the-shelf figure.<br>3. <b>The rule in one sentence:</b> the comparison column is what the item will cost on the shelf, from each supplier, at the comparison quantity — anything else compares fragments.</p><p><b>3. The other columns.</b> Price is one criterion:</p><p>1. <b>Lead time</b> — quoted, and adjusted by the supplier's measured reality where module 8's data exists (a quoted two weeks from a supplier whose measured performance is four is entered as quoted with the measured figure beside it).<br>2. <b>Payment terms</b> — the working-capital value of offered terms, computed per the accountant's arithmetic where terms differ materially.<br>3. <b>Quality and performance</b> — the scorecard figures (module 8) for known suppliers: on-time rate, damage rate, dispute history. New suppliers carry their verification status and any trial-order condition.<br>4. <b>Minimums, breaks, and validity</b> — stated per quote, with the break tier at the comparison quantity marked.</p><p><b>4. The total-cost view.</b> Where criteria pull in different directions, the worksheet computes the comparable total: the landed rate at the comparison quantity, adjusted for the terms value, with the risk criteria (lead time, performance) stated beside the money rather than converted into it by invented weights. The decision (Chapter 5) weighs the stated figures; the worksheet's job is to state them honestly and completely, one page per item, readable by an approver who was not in the cycle.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The award decision", "12", "<p>The comparison feeds a decision. This chapter is the criteria, the record, and what the award updates.</p><p><b>1. The decision criteria.</b> Per the written policy, the award goes to the best total position, not reflexively to the lowest rate:</p><p>1. <b>The landed figure at the real quantity</b> — the money criterion, from the worksheet's centre column.<br>2. <b>Supply reliability</b> — the measured performance and lead time against the demand's dates: the lowest rate that arrives late costs its saving in stock-outs, and the policy states how reliability is weighed for the class (essential lines weigh it heavily; discretionary lines less).<br>3. <b>Terms and relationship structure</b> — the payment terms' value, the arrangement potential (Chapter 6), and the concentration position (module 1's read: an award that deepens a named dependency is made knowingly or not at all).</p><p><b>2. Split awards.</b> The decision may split: the volume line to the best landed position, a secondary share to the qualified alternative — the deliberate dual-sourcing the concentration review recommends on critical classes, priced as the policy allows (the secondary share may carry a premium, stated in the minute as the cost of supply security). A split is a decision with its reasoning, not an inability to decide.</p><p><b>3. The decision minute.</b> One page, filed with the worksheet:</p><p>1. The award: supplier(s), items, rates, quantities or bands, terms, validity.<br>2. The reasoning: the criteria as weighed for this decision, in two or three sentences.<br>3. The alternatives: what the unsuccessful quotes offered, in one line each — the record that makes next cycle's negotiation informed and this cycle's decision defensible.<br>4. The approval: per the matrix's band for the award's value.</p><p><b>4. What the award updates.</b></p><p>1. <b>The buying lists</b> — the awarded rates load by the governed update with the minute as their source: module 2's sourced-rates rule closing its loop.<br>2. <b>The demand pipeline</b> — module 5's Purchase Order creates against the screened demand at the awarded rates; the trace runs request → worksheet → award → order.<br>3. <b>The supplier file</b> — the award, and equally the non-award, files per supplier: module 8's relationship review reads both.</p><p><b>5. Unsuccessful suppliers.</b> Informed per the policy's courtesy standard — the relationship the business may need next cycle is maintained by the professionalism of this one. The notification's content follows the policy (the fact of the award; commercial details per the confidentiality rules), and the response-rate read expects continued participation as its measure of whether the cycle treats its participants well.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Negotiation and standing arrangements", "12", "<p>The comparison creates a negotiating position; repeat lines convert awards into arrangements. This chapter is both.</p><p><b>1. The negotiation round.</b> Between comparison and award, the policy permits a negotiation round with the leading suppliers:</p><p>1. <b>Conducted from the worksheet</b> — the position is the recorded alternatives; the asks are specific (the rate at the volume tier, the terms, the included delivery) and minuted as asked and answered.<br>2. <b>The level-field rule</b> — the round's structure follows the policy (best-and-final from the shortlist, or sequential negotiation as the policy allows); one supplier's quote is not disclosed to another, because a cycle that leaks quotes teaches suppliers to quote high and wait.<br>3. <b>The outcome enters the record</b> — negotiated improvements amend the Supplier Quotation (the supplier's confirming document attached) and the worksheet re-states before the award minute writes.</p><p><b>2. Volume bands.</b> The negotiation's standing instrument for repeat lines: the rate agreed per quantity band — the tier structure entered from Chapter 3 becoming the agreed schedule. The consolidated ordering (module 3) then prices predictably: the 300-carton window lands in its band at its rate, and the ordering arithmetic can deliberately reach a band where the worksheet shows the crossing pays.</p><p><b>3. Standing arrangements.</b> For lines bought repeatedly, the award converts into an arrangement — the agreed rates (or bands), terms, and validity period, recorded per the site's instrument (the supplier-specific price structure, or the buying-side blanket order where quantities commit):</p><p>1. <b>Inside validity, purchases execute without re-quoting</b> — the module 1 exception operating as designed: the cycle ran once at the arrangement's scale.<br>2. <b>The validity is real</b> — expiry triggers the re-quote cadence (below); rates do not drift past their period by inattention, per the same rule as the selling side's blanket orders.<br>3. <b>Price-change requests inside validity</b> — the supplier's increase letter routes to the review: accepted changes are dated amendments with the notice filed; the trend read (module 2) shows what moved and when.</p><p><b>4. The re-quote cadence.</b> Per class in the policy: annual for stable domestic lines, semi-annual where markets move, per-consignment elements on import lines (freight re-quotes with the market even where goods rates hold). The cadence is calendared with the quarterly cycle reads, so re-quoting is scheduled work rather than a response to a supplier's letter — the business that only re-quotes when a supplier raises prices has ceded the calendar to its suppliers.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The cycle reads", "12", "<p>The standing reads on the quotation cycle, each with its cadence and action.</p><p><b>1. The open-cycle read — weekly.</b> Open Requests for Quotation by response deadline and age: reminders to non-responders at the midpoint; cycles past deadline closed and compared; cycles stalled before award chased to their blocking step — a demand pipeline waiting on a stalled cycle is a stock-out forming upstream, and the read connects the two dates.</p><p><b>2. The validity read — weekly.</b> Supplier Quotations and arrangements by days-to-expiry: awards pending against expiring quotes decided before the clock runs out (an award made against an expired quote is a re-quote conducted involuntarily); arrangements approaching expiry entering the re-quote calendar per Chapter 6.</p><p><b>3. The response-rate read — quarterly.</b> Per supplier: requests sent, responses received, response timeliness. The patterns route: the reliable responder is the relationship working; the chronic non-responder is a module 8 conversation (and, unresolved, a register question — a supplier that will not quote is a supplier in name); a class where response rates fall across suppliers is a market signal the review reads.</p><p><b>4. The award pattern read — quarterly.</b> The quarter's awards by supplier, class, and value:</p><p>1. <b>The competitiveness figure</b> — cycles with the policy's quote count achieved, against cycles that ran short (with reasons: genuine single-source, or a supplier base too thin — the widening action).<br>2. <b>The concentration movement</b> — awards' effect on the module 1 concentration figures, read together with the risk review.<br>3. <b>The incumbency pattern</b> — the incumbent's win rate: a rate near one hundred percent with prices drifting upward is the signal that the cycle has become a formality, and the deliberate-widening action (new qualified suppliers into the next cycle) is its correction.</p><p><b>5. The savings record — quarterly.</b> The cycle's measured contribution: negotiated improvements against first quotes, band crossings that paid, and the avoided-cost figures where re-quoting moved an incumbent's drift back — stated in naira with the cycles referenced, filed with the procurement function's quarterly records alongside module 3's purchases-avoided figure. The two figures together are the demand-and-sourcing machinery's stated value, in money, per quarter — the record the owner's review reads when the function's cost is weighed against its contribution.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: establishing the Emzor arrangement", "13", "<p>This chapter runs the cycle that produced the ₦9,600 rate the module 3 consolidation priced from. Values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>The trigger.</b> PARA-500's volume and the class policy (annual arrangements for core pharmaceutical lines) put the line into the annual quotation cycle: the consolidated annual volume justifies arrangement-scale negotiation rather than order-by-order pricing.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the request issues (Chapter 2).</b> The Request for Quotation goes to three registered suppliers: <b>Emzor</b>, the <b>second domestic manufacturer</b>, and the <b>importer channel</b> as the qualified alternative. The items table carries the annual volume structure and the delivery destination (Central Warehouse); the message asks for price breaks by quantity band, payment terms, lead times, and validity; the deadline is set with the mid-point reminder calendared.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the quotations enter (Chapter 3).</b> All three respond. Each Supplier Quotation enters from the supplier's attached document, rates paired to units (per Carton-12 throughout), break structures recorded as tiers — including Emzor's tiering with its band at the 300-carton volume level. The importer's quote notes ex-works terms; the clarification round confirms it in writing.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the comparison (Chapter 4).</b> The worksheet compares landed at the annual and per-window quantities: the domestic manufacturers' included-delivery rates stand nearly as quoted; the importer's rate gains the acquisition layer and loses its apparent advantage — the landed column re-ordering the ranking the raw rates suggested. The other columns fill: lead times (with measured history beside Emzor's quote), terms offered, and the scorecard figures for the incumbents.</p><p><b>Step 4 — negotiation and award (Chapters 5-6).</b> The negotiation round runs from the worksheet with the two domestic manufacturers: the specific asks — the band rate and terms — minuted as asked and answered. The award decision: <b>Emzor</b> takes the volume line at <b>₦9,600 per Carton-12 at the volume band</b>, on <b>30-day terms</b>, with the second manufacturer holding a secondary share on the overlap range — the split stated in the minute as the concentration mitigation the module 1 risk review recommended, with its premium visible. The decision minute files with the worksheet: award, reasoning in three sentences, alternatives in a line each, approval per the band.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the award updates the machinery (Chapters 5-6).</b> The rates load onto the buying list by the governed update, the minute as source; the arrangement records with its validity and the re-quote date calendared; the unsuccessful importer is informed per the courtesy standard and remains a responsive participant in the read. When module 3's consolidation later sums 300 cartons, the worksheet prices from this arrangement without re-quoting — the band rate applying exactly as agreed — and module 5's order will commit it.</p><p><b>The cycle evidence.</b> The request with its message; three quotations with attached documents; the worksheet with its landed centre; the negotiation minutes; the decision minute with alternatives; the sourced rate on the list; the calendar entry for re-quote. The rate every PARA-500 order carries is answerable back to this file in one lookup — the negotiation memory the module exists to build.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in the quotation cycle, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the ex-works comparison.</b> Symptom: rankings built on raw quoted rates; the import line awarded on a figure that omits its acquisition layer; the shelf cost discovered after commitment. Correction: the worksheet rebuilt landed; the award reviewed. Prevention: the landed column as the comparison's centre, per the module 2 rule.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the unverifiable rate.</b> Symptom: quotes entered from calls with no confirming document; awards that cannot show what the supplier actually offered; disputes at the first invoice. Correction: written confirmation obtained or the quote excluded. Prevention: entry from attached documents, and phone rates confirmed in writing before comparison closes.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the leaked cycle.</b> Symptom: one supplier's rates disclosed to another; suppliers learning to quote high and wait for the leak; the comparison's integrity gone. Correction: the round's records reviewed; the policy's structure re-imposed. Prevention: the level-field rule, and negotiation conducted from the worksheet's recorded position rather than from disclosures.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the formality cycle.</b> Symptom: the incumbent winning every cycle at drifting prices; requests issued to the same short list; the comparison a ritual before a foregone award. Correction: the deliberate widening — new qualified suppliers into the next cycle. Prevention: the award pattern read's incumbency figure, acted on.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the undocumented award.</b> Symptom: rates on orders with no minute behind them; decisions that cannot state their criteria; the next negotiation starting from nothing. Correction: the record reconstructed where evidence allows. Prevention: the decision minute as the award's required form, and the trace audit expecting it.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the expired arrangement still pricing.</b> Symptom: purchases at arrangement rates past validity; increases accepted without dated amendments; the calendar ceded to supplier letters. Correction: the re-quote run; rates re-established. Prevention: the validity read and the calendared re-quote cadence per class.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state when the policy requires the cycle and the recorded exceptions; issue a complete request with breaks, terms, and deadlines asked; enter quotations from documents with the pairing rule and tiers; build the worksheet with the landed centre and the stated criteria; run the award with its minute, splits, and updates; conduct the negotiation round and maintain arrangements with real validity; and run the five cycle reads with the savings record.</p><p><b>Module rule 4: a quoted rate is evidence — asked completely, entered from the supplier's document, compared landed at the real quantity, awarded by stated criteria in a filed minute with its alternatives, loaded to the lists with its source, and re-quoted on the calendar the business owns.</b> Module 5 covers the Purchase Order.</p>"),
]

